@title Product Design Principles
@subtitle Fathom Bank, Design Systems team
@author Design Systems team, Fathom Bank

These principles were agreed in a workshop with 26 designers and engineers on 2026-09-17. They apply to all customer-facing apps.

## Clarity over cleverness

Customers should understand what a control does before they touch it. Plain labels beat clever icons, especially where money moves. When in doubt, say the action out loud.

- Do: label buttons with the action, such as "Send transfer".
- Avoid: icons without text for money movement.

## Show the money

Fees, limits and totals belong in front of the customer before they commit. A customer who learns the real cost on the last screen will feel tricked, even if the fee was disclosed somewhere.

- Do: show fees before confirmation.
- Avoid: revealing fees on the final screen.

## Forgive mistakes

People make mistakes, and the product should make them recoverable. A customer who schedules the wrong transfer should be able to take it back without calling anyone.

- Do: allow undo for 10 seconds after a transfer is scheduled.
- Avoid: making cancellation a phone call.

## Accessible by default

Accessibility is part of the design, not a later pass. Color should support meaning, never carry it alone, and text should be readable for everyone who uses the app.

- Do: meet a contrast ratio of 4.5 to 1.
- Avoid: relying on color alone for status.

## Calm, not urgent

Customers make better decisions when they are not rushed. State deadlines plainly, and let people take the time they need.

- Do: state plain deadlines.
- Avoid: countdown timers that push decisions.

> "If a customer has to read it twice, we wrote it wrong."
> — Ines Albrecht, Head of Design

! Disagreements with a principle go to the monthly design council, not to individual reviews.
